WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.880
The buzz is fixed.

2
00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.100
Then a new ticket arrives:

3
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.160
I can hear myself.

4
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.220
Every word comes back to me,

5
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.160
faint, a moment later,

6
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.440
like talking in a canyon.

7
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.100
You ring a test line and say hello.

8
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.480
A beat later,
quieter and a little blurry: hello.

9
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.060
You time the gap: a quarter of a second.

10
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.600
At eight thousand samples a second,

11
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.160
that's two thousand samples.

12
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.200
Far down your line,

13
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.860
a box on a street corner in another city
leaks a little of the caller's voice back.

14
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:22.780
You can't open it.

15
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.160
But if you can predict the echo,

16
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.280
you can subtract it.

17
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.610
And the box passes lesson one's two tests.

18
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.310
Doubling the input doubles the echo,

19
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.150
and two inputs added
give their echoes added,

20
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.030
so it's linear.

21
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.730
Delaying the input only delays the echo,

22
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.090
so it's time-invariant.

23
00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.270
So what's the simplest
thing you could send?

24
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:39.810
Not a word, and not a tone.

25
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.530
A single click: one sample at one,

26
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.110
and every other sample at zero.

27
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.890
That signal is called an impulse.

28
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.150
Send it into the echo path
and record what comes back.

29
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.030
That's the impulse response, written h.

30
00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.630
Silence for two thousand samples,

31
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.190
then point four, then point two,

32
00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:54.370
then silence for good.

33
00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.790
Can two sizes and a delay really predict
the echo of a whole sentence?

34
00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.330
Try a very short word.

35
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.270
A caller's “hi” lasts four samples:

36
00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:03.190
point eight, point four,

37
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.070
minus point six, minus point two.

38
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.890
You only know what the box
does to a click,

39
00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.070
so turn the word into clicks:

40
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.790
each sample is a click of its own size,

41
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:10.830
at its own place.

42
00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:11.590
Add the four clicks,

43
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.770
and you get the word back.

44
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.820
Now each click launches its own copy of h.

45
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.960
Linearity scales it by the click's size.

46
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.100
Time-invariance starts it
as many samples late as its click.

47
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.840
Add the copies, sample by sample,

48
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.080
and out comes the echo:

49
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.820
five samples, starting at two thousand,

50
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.140
peaking at point three two.

51
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.000
One click just predicted the echo
of a word you never sent.

52
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.100
That's convolution:

53
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.120
building the output as a sum
of shifted copies of the impulse response,

54
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.600
each scaled by one input sample.

55
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.980
The book writes it x star h,

56
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.680
and that star is not multiplication.

57
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.680
Multiply “hi” by h sample by sample,

58
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.140
and every product has a zero in it.

59
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.260
No echo at all.

60
00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:45.850
Here's the fix.

61
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.310
Your equipment saw every sample
of the caller's voice,

62
00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.310
and you measured h.

63
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.770
So compute the echo yourself:

64
00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.510
point four times the voice
two thousand samples back,

65
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.610
plus point two times the voice
two thousand and one samples back.

66
00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.430
Subtract that guess from what arrives.

67
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.330
The echo is gone, and the far voice
comes through at full size.

68
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.150
Callers stop stammering.

69
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.230
Then a new complaint:

70
00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:06.310
everyone sounds muffled,

71
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.770
like they're talking through a wall.

72
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.870
The line is linear and time-invariant,

73
00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.070
so nothing was invented.

74
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.990
Something was taken away. But what?
